      "description": "**Question.** How weak can a motion signal be and still have its direction seen? (A human version of the monkeys' task.)\n\n**On screen.** A fixation point, then [[param:stimulus_duration]] of random dots in a black disc around it. A share of the dots, the correlation, steps left or right together; the rest jump at random.\n\n**The subject.** Looks at the fixation point (asked, not checked) and, after the dots, presses the left or right arrow. The point turns green or red.\n\n**Measured.** Proportion correct at each correlation ([[param:coherences_pct]] %), fitted for threshold.\n\n**Limits.** No eye tracker, so eye movements are not excluded. No MT recording, so no neuron comparison.",
      "sourceFiles": [
        "src/britten1992/task.py",
        "src/britten1992/rdk.py",
        "configs/task.yaml",
        "configs/rig-booth.yaml"
      ],
      "notes": [
        "Adaptation: the paper tested monkeys reporting with a saccade; keys, the 500-ms fixation period, 300-ms feedback and 1-s interval are ASSUMED (configs/task.yaml).",
        "Axis (horizontal), speed 6 dva/s, aperture 10 dva and the correlation set are ASSUMED; the paper tailored them to each MT neuron.",
        "Dot count is one frame at the booth rig's 60 Hz: 16.7 dots/deg²/s over a 20 x 20 dva plotting field, of which only dots inside the aperture are drawn.",
        "Inks: yellow fixation point (#ffff00, paper p. 4748), white dots and targets, black aperture disc, on the window's mid-grey (the paper's whole display was dark: a deviation in the config).",
        "Human set, no eye tracker: fixation is instructed only, so eye movements toward or with the dots are not excluded (unlike the paper's search-coil-enforced 1.25° window)."
      ]
    },
